# Multi-actor git hygiene (firm rules)

Agent Fleet runs, Claude Code sessions, and other Cursor windows can all merge to `dev` while
you work. A stale local `dev` has already caused a near-regression (2026-07-21). Follow these
without exception:

- **Always `git fetch` first and branch from `origin/dev`, never local `dev`.** Rebase onto
  `origin/dev` immediately before opening a PR.
- **One actor per working tree.** If another session/tool is working in this clone, take your
  own git worktree first: `git worktree add ../<repo>-cursor dev` and work there.
- **Never commit directly on `dev`** in a fleet-enabled repo (one with a `dev-agent` branch) —
  branch + PR always; the fleet merges to `dev` underneath you.
- **Never push to an existing remote branch name you didn't create in this session** — check
  `git ls-remote origin <name>` when unsure.
- `dev` may carry a ruleset (PR required, no force pushes; the fleet app bypasses it). If a
  push is rejected, that is the guardrail working — open a PR instead of forcing.

**If commits are stuck on local `dev`** (made before these rules, now rejected on push):
`git branch rescue/dev-work` (saves them — nothing is lost), then `git fetch origin` and
`git checkout dev && git reset --hard origin/dev`, then open a PR from `rescue/dev-work`.
NOTE: `reset --hard` discards UNCOMMITTED changes — commit or stash first.

## Wrong-identity guard (gh / git tokens)

Before any repo-mutating `gh` or git operation, know WHO you are: `gh auth status` (or
`gh api user --jq .login`). If a `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` env var is set, it OVERRIDES
keyring login - a limited machine token (e.g. a CI account) will 404 on repos a human can
plainly see in the browser, producing misleading "not found"/"branch missing" errors.
When that happens, do not chase the phantom error; retry as the human identity:
`env -u GH_TOKEN -u GITHUB_TOKEN <command>`. Never push/commit as a machine identity when
the work should be attributed to the developer (fleet notifications key off the author).
